{"format_version":1,"partition":5435,"records":{"bau":{"entries":[["bau",null,"/baʊ̯/","From Middle High German bū, bou, from Old High German bū, ultimately from Proto-Germanic *būą (“building, dwelling”).\nThe plural form Bauten is that of the related Low German Bude (“building”), from Middle Low German bûwete. The singular Baute did not catch on in standard German.",{"g":"m","decl":"||/e|es/s|ten/e|ten/e|ten/en|ten/e"}]],"senses":[[17869,"bau","noun","building, construction (the act of constructing)",0,"m"],[17870,"bau","noun","building site, construction site (place where such constructing takes place)",1,"m"],[17871,"bau","noun","building, construction, edifice (built-up structure, house)",2,"m"],[17872,"bau","noun","burrow (of an animal, especially underground)",3,"m"],[17873,"bau","noun","prison, jail",4,"m",["colloquial"]],[17874,"bau","verb","singular imperative of bauen",5]],"inflections":[["bau","bauen","imperative,second-person,singular"]]},"begrenzteres":{"entries":[["begrenzteres",null,null,null]],"senses":[[711833,"begrenzteres","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative neuter singular comparative degree of begrenzt",0]],"inflections":[["begrenzteres","begrenzt","comparative,neuter,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"blutkreislaufs":{"entries":[["blutkreislaufs",null,null,null]],"senses":[[234651,"blutkreislaufs","noun","genitive singular of Blutkreislauf",0]],"inflections":[["blutkreislaufs","blutkreislauf","genitive"]]},"dadaistischstes":{"entries":[["dadaistischstes",null,null,null]],"senses":[[459847,"dadaistischstes","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative neuter singular superlative degree of dadaistisch",0]],"inflections":[["dadaistischstes","dadaistisch","neuter,nominative,singular,strong,superlative,without-article"]]},"dolch":{"entries":[["dolch",null,"/dɔlç/","Uncertain. First attested in the mid-15th century in Upper German texts as Dolch, then also Dollich, Tolch. At first mostly with weak declension (dem Dolchen). The formerly held assumption of a Slavic origin has been abandoned (older Czech tulich is instead a German borrowing).\nThe word is now mostly derived ultimately from Latin dolo (“pike, dagger”), but the origin of the velar remains unclear. It may be due to conflation with a descendant of Proto-West Germanic *dalk, *dolk (“pin, needle, clasp”), which is entirely unattested in Continental West Germanic, only being found in Old English dalc, dolc (“brooch, buckle, clasp”)). Another theory links it to Middle French dollequin (ca. 1400), from Old French dolequin, itself likely from Middle Dutch *dollekijn, a diminutive of Middle Dutch dolle (“dagger”, mid-14th c.), likely from the Latin. Compare early Dutch dolleken, dolck, dolch (modern Dutch dolk).",{"g":"m","decl":"||/e|es/s|e|e|en|e"}]],"senses":[[6672,"dolch","noun","dagger",0,"m"]]},"dorschen":{"entries":[["dorschen",null,null,null]],"senses":[[352964,"dorschen","noun","dative plural of Dorsch",0,"m"]],"inflections":[["dorschen","dorsch","dative,definite,plural"]]},"drehgeschwindigkeit":{"entries":[["drehgeschwindigkeit",null,"[ˈdʁeːɡəˌʃvɪndɪçkaɪ̯t]","From drehen + Geschwindigkeit.",{"g":"f","decl":"||||en|en|en|en"}]],"senses":[[715467,"drehgeschwindigkeit","noun","angular velocity",0,"f"]]},"einstweiligem":{"entries":[["einstweiligem",null,null,null]],"senses":[[263275,"einstweiligem","adj","strong dative masculine/neuter singular of einstweilig",0]],"inflections":[["einstweiligem","einstweilig","dative,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"entenkeulen":{"entries":[["entenkeulen",null,"/ˈɛntənˌkɔɪ̯lən/",null]],"senses":[[717330,"entenkeulen","noun","plural of Entenkeule",0]],"inflections":[["entenkeulen","entenkeule","plural"]]},"fluchtwilligere":{"entries":[["fluchtwilligere",null,null,null]],"senses":[[470294,"fluchtwilligere","adj","inflection of fluchtwillig:",0],[470295,"fluchtwilligere","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular comparative degree",1],[470296,"fluchtwilligere","adj","strong